<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="manifest-format" title="The weave manifest">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        A module that declares a weave or an extension ships one JSON document. It sits at
        <path>META-INF/aether/weaves.json</path>. Below is a whole one, holding a weave with a
        shadowed field, one injector, and one extension holder.
        <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> covers who writes it.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="json" src="weaves.json"/>

    <chapter title="The root object" id="root">
        <p>Four keys, in this order.</p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Key</td><td>Absent means</td><td>Read back by</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>version</code></td><td><code>1</code>, and a higher one is refused</td>
                <td>Before any entry</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>generator</code></td><td>the text <code>unknown</code></td>
                <td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>weaves</code></td><td>an empty array</td>
                <td>A driver, per class named</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>extensions</code></td><td>an empty array</td>
                <td>The engine, and <code>stubs</code></td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            A higher version is refused whole, as <code>AW2301</code>. Rebuild that artefact
            against this release, or upgrade %product%.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="A weave entry" id="weave">
        <p>
            Nine keys, and only <code>class</code> decides anything. A reader takes the class name
            and nothing else. <a href="weaving-model.topic">The weaving model</a> lists what
            the parser then finds on the class. Four of the other eight — <code>kind</code>,
            <code>priority</code>, <code>targets</code> and <code>injectors</code> — are read back
            only by the <a href="tooling.topic">IntelliJ IDEA plugin</a>, which lists a
            dependency's weaves. The remaining four are read by nothing.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Key</td><td>Absent means</td><td>Read back by</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>class</code></td><td>the entry is dropped</td><td>Every reader</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td><code>INSTANCE</code></td><td>IntelliJ IDEA</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>priority</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td>IntelliJ IDEA</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>require</code></td><td><code>REQUIRED</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>phase</code></td><td><code>DEFAULT</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tags</code></td><td>an empty array</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>targets</code></td><td>an empty array</td><td>IntelliJ IDEA</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>members</code></td><td>an empty array</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>injectors</code></td><td>an empty array</td><td>Two of its keys</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            An entry naming no class is dropped on its own, as <code>AW2300</code>. Rebuild the
            artefact holding it. <code>kind</code>, <code>require</code> and <code>phase</code>
            carry a constant's name as free text. Nothing checks them against the enumeration.
            <a href="ordering.topic">Ordering</a> covers what <code>priority</code> and
            <code>phase</code> mean.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Members" id="members">
        <p>
            A member is a field or method that is not a handler. A handler becomes an injector
            instead, so one method never appears twice.
            <a href="weave-members.topic">Weave members</a> covers the four dispositions.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Key</td><td>Absent means</td><td>Read back by</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>disposition</code></td><td><code>MERGE</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td><code>FIELD</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>reading throws</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>descriptor</code></td><td>the empty string</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>targetName</code></td><td>the value of <code>name</code></td>
                <td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>unique</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            A member's <code>targetName</code> differs from its <code>name</code> wherever
            <code>@Shadow</code>, <code>@Accessor</code> or <code>@Invoker</code> writes a
            non-blank value. It also differs where an unnamed accessor strips a
            <code>get</code>, <code>set</code> or <code>is</code> prefix, or an unnamed invoker
            strips <code>call</code> or <code>invoke</code>. A prefix counts only when followed by
            an upper-case letter, then lower-cased: <code>getLedger</code> yields
            <code>ledger</code>, but <code>setup</code> stays <code>setup</code>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Injectors and points" id="injectors">
        <p>
            One injector is one handler declaration. Its points are the places inside the target
            method. <a href="annotations.topic">Annotations</a> has the declarations these come
            from.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Key</td><td>Absent means</td><td>Read back by</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td><code>INJECT</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>the text <code>unnamed</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>handler</code></td><td>the injector is dropped</td>
                <td>IntelliJ IDEA</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>method</code></td><td>the injector is dropped</td>
                <td>IntelliJ IDEA</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>points</code></td><td>an empty array</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>require</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>allow</code></td><td><code>0</code>, no upper bound</td>
                <td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>group</code></td><td>the empty string</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            A handler is a method name with its descriptor straight after it. That names one
            overload exactly. <code>method</code> is the target selector. It is canonical only
            where the descriptor form names its owning class and every part resolves. The source
            form, which never names one, is never canonical.
            <a href="selector-grammar.topic">The selector grammar</a> has both forms.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Key</td><td>Absent means</td><td>Read back by</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>point</code></td><td><code>HEAD</code>, and blank throws</td>
                <td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>target</code></td><td>the empty string</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ordinal</code></td><td><code>-1</code>, every match</td>
                <td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>shift</code></td><td><code>NONE</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>by</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>access</code></td><td><code>ANY</code></td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>slice</code></td><td>the empty string</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            A point's <code>target</code> is stored exactly as written. Nothing canonicalises it.
            <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> has the identifiers
            <code>point</code> may hold.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="An extension entry" id="extensions">
        <p>
            Extensions live in their own array. No weave entry mentions one. Every key of an
            extension is read back. The last four are written only where they differ from the
            default beside them. <a href="extension-methods.topic">Extension methods</a> explains
            the three shapes of call.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Key</td><td>Absent means</td><td>Read back by</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>class</code></td><td>the entry is dropped</td>
                <td>Every reader</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>receiver</code></td><td>the entry is dropped</td>
                <td>Every reader</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>the entry is dropped</td><td>Every reader</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>descriptor</code></td><td>the entry is dropped</td>
                <td>Every reader</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td><code>instance</code></td>
                <td>The engine and IntelliJ IDEA</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>require</code></td><td><code>REQUIRED</code></td>
                <td>The <code>stubs</code> goal</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>nulls</code></td><td><code>UNCHECKED</code></td><td>The engine</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>scope</code></td><td><code>PUBLIC</code></td>
                <td>The <code>stubs</code> goal</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            Here <code>kind</code> holds a lower-case token. A weave's enumerated keys hold a
            constant's name in capitals. The descriptor is the call site's, so an instance
            extension leaves its receiver out.
            <a href="extension-methods.topic">Extension methods</a> covers what <code>require</code>,
            <code>nulls</code> and <code>scope</code> each do.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                Merging matches an extension by its holder class alone. Split one holder across two
                manifests and half of it is lost. Keep a holder's declarations in one module.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The JSON this reads" id="json">
        <p>
            A manifest arrives from whatever is on the classpath. So the parser accepts more than
            the writer ever emits.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Accepted, though never written">
                <p>
                    A JSON <code>null</code>, a leading zero, and a duplicate key with the last one
                    winning. The escapes for a solidus, a backspace and a form feed are read too.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Ignored without a word">
                <p>
                    An unknown key, at any level. A key of the wrong JSON type reads as absent, and
                    so does a <code>null</code>. An array element of the wrong shape is dropped. So
                    is a non-string in <code>tags</code> or <code>targets</code>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Refused">
                <p>
                    Comments, a trailing comma, a single-quoted string and a byte-order mark. So
                    are a fraction, an exponent, and any non-whitespace after the closing brace.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Bounded">
                <p>
                    Nesting stops at 64 objects and arrays. The sample above reaches seven. A
                    number outside <code>long</code> range is refused as <code>AW2300</code>; one
                    within <code>long</code> range but outside <code>int</code> range is truncated
                    rather than reported.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            Keys are written in a fixed order, and nothing hash-ordered is iterated. One manifest
            renders to the same bytes on every machine.
            <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> covers what that buys you.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What damage costs" id="damage">
        <p>What survives depends on what is wrong.</p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>What is wrong</td><td>What survives</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Not a JSON object at all</td>
                <td>Nothing. <code>AW2300</code>; rebuild it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A <code>version</code> above <code>1</code></td>
                <td>Nothing. <code>AW2301</code>; rebuild it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A weave entry with no <code>class</code></td>
                <td>Every other entry</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An injector with no <code>handler</code></td>
                <td>Its weave's other injectors</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An extension missing a key</td>
                <td>Every other entry</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An unknown extension <code>kind</code></td>
                <td>Every other entry</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An unknown extension policy</td>
                <td>That entry, at its default</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A blank <code>name</code> or <code>point</code></td>
                <td>Nothing, and no later one</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            The last row has no code behind it. A <code>version</code> below zero behaves the same
            way. Reading throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>. In the reactor nothing
            catches it and discovery stops there; IntelliJ IDEA catches it per manifest instead,
            dropping only that library's listing. Rebuild that artefact.
            <a href="diagnostics-environment.topic">AW2xxx</a> has what each code means.
        </p>
    </chapter>

